          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal declarative directly avows Goneril’s loving emotional state toward Lear. Its core linguistic force is truth-conditional presentation of that love, regardless of whether the avowal is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not undertake a future action, obligation, or relational commitment. Lear’s decision to distribute bounty according to the daughters’ answers makes the avowal consequential, but it does not convert Goneril’s assertion of present love into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is a syntactically complete assertion delivered as a deliberate answer to Lear’s question. The surrounding hyperbolic rhetoric intensifies the claim but does not show a reflex-like or affective verbal discharge distinct from avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No additional core force beyond the avowal is established for the exact focal expression. Its strategic role in the competition for Lear’s bounty belongs to the speech situation rather than constituting a separate illocutionary mode in “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers with the size of his bounty. Goneril, called upon first, addresses Lear with the focal avowal and then elaborates it through comparisons and hyperbole.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril makes no identifiable undertaking in the focal expression. Lear treats her wider answer as satisfying his request and immediately grants territory to her and Albany’s line, but that is uptake of an avowal in his bounty-allocation procedure, not acceptance of a promise. The supplied scene contains Goneril’s emphatic profession as evidence favoring affection, while the material incentive and Cordelia’s skeptical contrast complicate sincerity; actual affection is therefore not settled.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The exact target is only “I love you.” The surrounding speech establishes its rhetorical and transactional setting but cannot by itself conclusively determine Goneril’s sincerity, private emotional state, or any unstated future intention.\"}"
